verly expressive architecture, so metrics like 24.9% CAGR at 7.9% MDD probably reflect overfitting capacity rather than genuine signal. The objective itself is also suspect: MDD on a single path is essentially a single-event statistic tied to one drawdown episode (GFC or COVID), making it extremely noisy and prone to being gamed by a genome that happens to dodge that one crash in training.\n\nThis also explains why risk metrics transport worse than returns — the GA directly optimized an extreme-order statistic (MDD) under heavy selection pressure, while CAGR averages over 20 years, so regression to the mean hits the minimized drawdown harder (a winner's curse effect). I should have anticipated this and pushed for a smoother tail-risk objective, like CVaR, average drawdown, Ulcer index, or MDD computed across bootstrapped/resampled sub-universes and start dates — defining robustness directly into the objective itself (e.g. median or 80th-percentile MDD over inner resamples) rather than treating it as an unapproved threshold guard.\n\nThe band structure itself compounds the problem: pushing finalists to sit exactly at band boundaries (like MDD hugging the bound in Fold 2) means any OOS degradation tips them over, and the tightest 10% band spanning 20 years with the GFC, COVID, and 2022 essentially demands near-perfect crash timing achievable only through overfitting — which is exactly why the 10% band finalists show the worst blind-test pathologies. On top of that, allowing absolute/raw feature levels (dollar volume, Amihud, raw volatility) opened a backdoor for stock-identity proxies, since thresholding on raw dollar volume essentially encodes \"is this a mega-cap.\"\n\nI should check whether the finalists actually relied on these raw features, since the 99% SAFE blind collapse in Fold1-F1 and Fold3-F1 fits the pattern of absolute thresholds tuned to training-set distributions that blind stocks simply don't match — whereas time-series percentile features, being stock